Antiviruses:
  Antiviruses are software that keeps devices secure by providing protection against malicious software (malware). Antiviruses can work in one of two ways: by scanning
  programs and files as they enter the device and comparing them to known viruses, or it can scan the files already on the device to try and locate any suspicious 
  behaviour. If an antivirus software detects potentially malicious software, it flags the file to be checked by the user, in case it's a false positive. If the software
  knows for sure that the file is malicious, it will automatically remove it from the device.
Virtual Private Networks (VPNs):
  VPNs are like regular browsers, except instead of routing your connection through your regular Internet Service Provider (ISP), it is routed through a private company.
  This masks the user's IP address. The VPN masks the location of the user, displaying a location set by the user. This provides security because the user cannot be 
  traced to their actual location by malicious users/ onlookers trying to gain sensitive information about users. Masking the IP address also prevents anyone who knows a
  user's IP address from viewing their search history, which could provide information to an attacker about compromising health-based or location-based conditions.
Password Managers:
  A password manager is a tool that allows you to create secure and unique passwords for all of your logins, while only needing to remember one password. The only 
  password that must be remembered is the login for the password manager itself. This can be a password, PIN or biometrics. They store all of your passwords, potentially
  featuring auto-fills to save time. Some more secure password managers feature additional methods to make them even more secure. For example, some feature encryption,
  to make the passwords harder for an attacker to get at. They can also feature two-factor authentication, ensuring that only the owner can access them.
Firewalls:
  A firewall is either a hardware device or a piece of software that helps protect the network from attackers. It is a filter that scans the data coming into the network
  preventing anything suspicious from entering. They do this by examining the data packets and comparing them to pre-defined rules. The rules are based on things like:
  source/ destination IP, ports and protocols. To enforce these rules, firewalls use techniques like packet filtering, proxy services, and stateful inspections.
  Packet filtering is the inspection of data packets as they travel through the firewall. 
  Proxy services are systems or routers that provides a connection between users and the internet.
  Stateful inspections monitor and maintain the context of active connections in order to make decisions about which packets to let through.
Vulnerability Scanners:
  Vulnerability scanners detect security weaknesses in systems and the software that runs the systems. First, vulnerability scanners create an inventory of assets. This
  includes operating systems, software, open ports and user accounts. Then, it scans the networks, hardware, software and systems to identify potential risk exposures
  and attack vectors. It will then compare these to known flaws. Any detected flaws/ weaknesses are then classified and reported on to help the organisation fix them.
  There are both authenticated, which require valid login credentials to the target system, and unauthenticated, which are performed outside the system and don't require
  logins.

